{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories in action is defensible without smuggling induction if framed as a non-inductive 'methodological bet' on the theory with the highest surviving informative content, where the cost of acceptance is the explicit renunciation of any claim that the theory is probably true or reliable for long-term prediction.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a guarantee of safety or reliability for high-stakes action (e.g., bridge building), Popper's framework cannot provide it without illicitly importing inductive assumptions about the stability of natural laws.","If the definition of 'verisimilitude' relies on comparing truth-content and falsity-content of false theories, the Tichý-Miller proof renders the comparative basis for preference logically incoherent.","If the 'methodological bet' implicitly assumes that a theory which survived severe tests yesterday is more likely to survive today, it collapses into the very induction Popper rejected."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'corroboration as a report of past performance' (which implies no future probability) and 'preference as a strategic choice to maximize the rate of error elimination.' By choosing the most severely tested theory, scientists maximize the chance of quickly falsifying a false theory, thereby accelerating progress toward truth (verisimilitude) without assuming the future will resemble the past. The preference is rational not because the theory is likely true, but because it is the most efficient vehicle for further testing.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}